{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<table><thead><tr><th>Helper</th><th>Arguments after the localizer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tl</code></td><td>key, formatting values</td></tr><tr><td><code>tn</code></td><td>singular key, plural key, count, formatting values</td></tr><tr><td><code>ctl</code></td><td>context, key, formatting values</td></tr><tr><td><code>ctn</code></td><td>context, singular key, plural key, count, formatting values</td></tr></tbody></table>
<pre><code>page := template.Must(template.New("welcome.gohtml").Funcs(tr.FuncMap()).ParseFiles("templates/welcome.gohtml"))</code></pre>
<pre><code>{{"{{"}} tl .Loc "Hello, %s" .Name }}
{{"{{"}} tl .Loc "%s has %d messages" .Name .Count }}
{{"{{"}} ctl .Loc "navigation" "Home" }}</code></pre>
<p>Go template arguments are separated by spaces. The translated string uses printf-style formatting: keep the placeholders and their expected types compatible across languages. Use indexed placeholders such as <code>%[2]d</code> when a translation needs to change the order.</p>
<h2>Calls from Go</h2>
<pre><code>greeting := tr.Tl(loc, "Hello, %s", name)
messages := tr.Tn(loc, "%d message", "%d messages", count, count)
label := tr.Ctl(loc, "navigation", "Home")</code></pre>
<p><code>Tl</code>, <code>Tn</code>, <code>Ctl</code> and <code>Ctn</code> take the same arguments as the template helpers; see <a href="{{ basePath }}/plurals" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/plurals" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">plural selection and formatting</a>. <code>ctl</code>/<code>Ctl</code> and <code>ctn</code>/<code>Ctn</code> without a context (<code>""</code>) behave exactly like <code>tl</code>/<code>Tl</code> and <code>tn</code>/<code>Tn</code>.</p>
<h2>Knowing whether a translation exists</h2>
<pre><code>text, found := tr.Lookup(loc, "Hello, %s", name)
if !found {
    // text is what SetUntranslated shows, by default "*Hello, %s*"
}</code></pre>
<p><code>Lookup</code>, <code>LookupN</code>, <code>LookupC</code> and <code>LookupCN</code> take the same arguments as <code>Tl</code>, <code>Tn</code>, <code>Ctl</code> and <code>Ctn</code>, return the same text, and also report whether a translation was found. Like the other lookups, they record the key when recording is on.</p>
<h2>Literal keys and escaping</h2>
<p>Use a stable literal key and pass values separately. Concatenating a name into the key creates a different catalogue entry for each value. <code>html/template</code> escapes the returned string for its HTML context; store ordinary text in translations and keep markup in templates.</p>
</article>
